INT. BELL TOWER - RUINED VILLAGE CHURCH - DAY (1944)

Broken stairs. A hole where the roof used to be. The great bell lies on its side, cracked, big as a bathtub. Dust drifts in a slant of light.

CORPORAL HADLEY (20s, helmet pushed back, rifle on his knee, a frayed notebook) sits on the bell's rim. He has been here since morning. He writes a line, crosses it out, writes another. Below, a mile off, a flat thud of artillery rolls over the hills, and he lifts his head, listens, and returns to the notebook.

HADLEY
(to himself)
Dear Mother. It is quiet here.

He sighs and tears out the page.

Footsteps on the stairs. Hadley raises the rifle. A figure climbs through the trapdoor, hands visible, helmet askew. NIKOLAI BRODSKY (30s, a medic with a red cross armband, a canvas bag of bandages slung across his chest) hauls himself up, coughing plaster.

NIKOLAI
Don't shoot. I'm one of yours.

HADLEY
Prove it.

NIKOLAI
I've got a bag of gauze and no sense of direction. Will that do?

Hadley lowers the rifle. Nikolai sits on the top stair, catches his breath, takes off his helmet and rubs his scalp. Hadley sets the notebook beside him on the bell, then shifts it to make room, then picks it up again, embarrassed.

HADLEY
Who sent you?

NIKOLAI
Nobody. My unit's gone. I followed the sound of the bell.

HADLEY
The bell doesn't ring. It's cracked.

NIKOLAI
It rang this morning. I heard it from the road.

HADLEY
That was me. I hit it with my boot.

Nikolai laughs. Hadley almost does.

NIKOLAI
How long have you been up here?

HADLEY
Since dawn. I was told to watch the road.

NIKOLAI
And?

HADLEY
Nothing. A cow. A priest, I think. A cart with a woman and a goat.

NIKOLAI
No Germans?

HADLEY
Not today.

Nikolai opens his bag, takes out a tin of biscuits, and passes one across. Hadley takes it, bites, chews, nods thanks. They sit like two men on a porch, with the war rumbling a long way off.

NIKOLAI
What were you writing?

HADLEY
A letter.

NIKOLAI
To whom?

HADLEY
My mother. I'm not very good at it.

NIKOLAI
What do you say?

HADLEY
That it's quiet.

NIKOLAI
Is it?

Hadley glances at the hole in the roof, the gray sky, a bird crossing it.

HADLEY
Right now.

Nikolai finishes his biscuit. He brushes crumbs from his knees, stands, and goes to the cracked bell, running a palm along its lip. He picks up a small stone from the floor.

NIKOLAI
May I?

HADLEY
Go ahead.

Nikolai taps the bell with the stone. A low, flat, broken note rolls out across the valley and dies.

Both of them wait to see if anyone answers. No one does.
